From henry.ward at me.com  Thu Jan  6 23:41:23 2011
From: henry.ward at me.com (Henry Ward)
Date: Thu, 06 Jan 2011 15:41:23 -0800
Subject: [Rpy] SOLUTION: Strange Failure calling robject.DataFrame() from Django that does not fail in Command Line
In-Reply-To: <C159E000-4022-454D-A8B3-888653754B3A@me.com>
References: <C159E000-4022-454D-A8B3-888653754B3A@me.com>
Message-ID: <35EEA056-7F05-48C1-9714-A358C4D8ABE3@me.com>

Hi all,
I've figured out part of the problem and the full solution and wanted to post the solution.  The problem is that the .rcall(kv, globalenv_ri) method uses the keys of the dictionary kv as the keyword arguments.  It was failing because the keyword arguments were in unicode and not string.  Apparently this is a bug in some versions of Python 2.6.  It seemed to work fine for me except with the Django webservice.  I still do not know why it works on all my interpreters except when running python manage.py runserver.  

But the quick fix is to manually cast the keywords in kv to string.  I made a minor modification to vectors.py  where I convert k into a string as str(k) inside the list iteration below.  This fixed the problem.

Thanks,

Henry

kv = [(str(k), conversion.py2ri(v)) for k,v in tlist.iteritems()]
            kv = tuple(kv)
            df = baseenv_ri.get("data.frame").rcall(kv, globalenv_ri)


